{"assets":["/editorial-assets/3ccb486ffaeda12f805a3af883cc191c6ecc68d152fbc13ae56389c0982b098c.png","/editorial-assets/ebe7dacc2a15d59270a91bba9032351e7b8294206aa1b5899d6fbc13c9b20d01.png","/editorial-assets/4bf0edd9ecfde6da5d3ea83a743dcb062927bb3e3e347cfda9fd63b0a065b49b.png","/editorial-assets/b7c36a6180df851060d051fbd94654d502a8a5cf69427a82d92af6ac467045a1.png","/editorial-assets/d0fbb58f35ae8f6a74cb84f4b606419fedf852a2538890a37bfdc5e4a61e8533.png","/editorial-assets/4df29e4f3eeaf9c6dcf881b66bc3b05c8716e39b7efaa5414147684c199e4c6c.png","/editorial-assets/7881a7507b1455e1eeccbaf09616abc04ee2f6dbd7de0866a194155a07e2b963.png","/editorial-assets/0eb0a68abf1ac29c1ae19f63a53588231bc145c446d2288ed55b65716ed95c3d.png","/editorial-assets/cc2b899ffc826206d73cd5b42b77de682b153f5e77b645cf90cd6fae26b56459.png","/editorial-assets/a84eca59fba198b9fc385c83aaa9d54bd4e59ef1efa3282315e1a299d38d9ae5.png","/editorial-assets/0f68288e604344adfca7b5366d236de04471d14b894cb0f225e1034b74eb33df.png","/editorial-assets/561493a0f93a9060a95453a2231d4b79b68fbb520e30fab75968f576dd200f47.png","/editorial-assets/3a0d388897ec6f1be7e9f4ef025fd650b3bc399edf104a69932c93277c227969.png"],"contestId":"724","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724A - Checking the Calendar"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Days of the week in two adjacent months may either be the same (in February and March), or differ by two (in April and May) or differ by three (in January and February). Also, it was necessary to pay attention to direction of that difference (monday and tuesday is not the same as tuesday and monday)."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724B - Batch Sort"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Order of swaps of numbers in each row is not important. In fact, the order is not important even when we will swap the entire columns (because we can always change the swaps in the rows so that the result will remain the same)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Therefore we can choose columns which we will swap (or choose not to swap them at all), and then for each row check if we can arrange the numbers in ascending order using only one swap."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This can be done by checking that all the numbers, except for maybe two of them, are already standing in their places."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724C - Ray Tracing"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's simulate the flight of the beam. It can be seen that for any segment between two successive reflections is true that the sum of the coordinates for each point of this segment is constant or the difference of the coordinates is constant."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Thus, we can make lists of sensors for each possible sum and difference of coordinates. Then, when we process the segment of the beam trajectory, we can take all the points from the corresponding list and update result for them."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This solution works fast, because each point is passed by the beam (and therefore viewed by us) not more than two times."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724D - Dense Subsequence"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It is not hard to see that if we choose some subset of the letters then to achieve lexicographically smallest string you must first write all of the letters 'a', then all of the letters 'b' and so on."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's assume that the string in the answer consists only of the letters 'a'. Then it should be as small of them as possible to get lexicographically smallest string. We can check whether it is enough to take only the letters 'a' to meet the requirement on the density of the indices (in each subsegment of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" it has to be at least one letter 'a'). If this is true, we can find the minimum number of letters 'a', which will satisfy the density requirement, using the greedy algorithm (every time we will take the rightmost letter 'a', which is separated from the previous one by no more than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" positions). If this is not true, then the answer must contain some other letters except for 'a'. This means that we need to take as much 'a' letters as possible. So we can take all of them, because adding any additional letter will not affect the density requirement."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then do the same with the letter 'b', bearing in mind that all of the letters 'a' is already taken. If the letters 'b' is not enough, then take all of the letters 'a', all of the letters 'b' and proceed to the next letter, and so on."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724E - Goods transportation"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Build a network of the following form: The network will consist of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2 vertices, the source located at the node with the number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 and the sink at the node with the number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2. For each node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" add the arc ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") with a capacity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and arc ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2) with capacity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Also, for each pair of nodes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that 1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", add the arc ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") with capacity "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The value of the maximum flow in this network is the answer to the problem. Since the network contains "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") arcs, the usual flow search algorithms is not applicable here."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Instead, we will find a capacity of the minimal cut in this network. The cut in this case is a partition of the set of all nodes from 1 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2 into two parts. Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the part that contains the source and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3ccb486ffaeda12f805a3af883cc191c6ecc68d152fbc13ae56389c0982b098c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"  — all other nodes. Then the capacity of the cut is equal to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ebe7dacc2a15d59270a91bba9032351e7b8294206aa1b5899d6fbc13c9b20d01.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Capacity of the minimal cut can be found using the following dynamic programming: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cut"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") will be the minimum capacity of the cut, which was built on the first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" nodes, such that the set "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" contains exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" nodes other than the source. It is easy to get the formula for this dynamic: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cut"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cut"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1) + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cut"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The answer to the problem can be obtained as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cut"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The resulting complexity of this solution is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). To fit in memory limit you should store only two consecutive rows during the computation of the dynamic."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724F - Uniformly Branched Trees"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At first, we will calculate the following dynamic programming: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"trees"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") will be the number of rooted trees, where the root has exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" directed subtrees, the maximum size of which does not exceed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and the degree of internal vertices is equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (except for the root)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Calculation of the value of the dynamics for fixed parameters is divided into two cases: "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" there are on subtrees of size "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" at all. In this case, you can take the value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"trees"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1); "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" there are "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 0 subtrees of size "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". In this case, we got "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4bf0edd9ecfde6da5d3ea83a743dcb062927bb3e3e347cfda9fd63b0a065b49b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" different trees. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The main idea here is that we do not distinguish trees that differ only by a permutation of the sons for some vertices (this is where the number of combinations come from). It's enough to set some order in which we will place the sons of all vertices. In this case, they are arranged in the order of ascending sizes and the index number of its subtrees (all the trees of fixed size can be written in one big list and enumerated)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we need to count the number of unrooted trees. Let's get rid of this restriction, by rooting all the trees using some vertex. For this vertex, we will take the centroid (the vertex, after removing which, the tree splits into connected components, with the sizes not larger than the half of the original size of the tree). The advantage of this choice is that any tree always have either one or two centroids."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The number of trees that have exactly one centroid is equal to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b7c36a6180df851060d051fbd94654d502a8a5cf69427a82d92af6ac467045a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". The fact that we chose the centroid, implies that the size of subtrees cannot exceed "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d0fbb58f35ae8f6a74cb84f4b606419fedf852a2538890a37bfdc5e4a61e8533.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The number of trees that have exactly two centroids is equal to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4df29e4f3eeaf9c6dcf881b66bc3b05c8716e39b7efaa5414147684c199e4c6c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is divisible by 2, and zero otherwise. If there are two centroids, they will be connected by an edge, and the removing of this edge will split the graph into two parts, each of which will contain exactly "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7881a7507b1455e1eeccbaf09616abc04ee2f6dbd7de0866a194155a07e2b963.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" vertices."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The resulting complexity of this solution is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"724G"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/724/problem/G"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"724G - Xor-matic Number of the Graph"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Without loss of generality, let's assume that the graph is connected. If not, we will calculate the answer for each connected component and then sum them up."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's сhoose two arbitrary vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and try to find all the interesting triples of the form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  ...). Let's find any path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", connecting these two vertices and calculate the xor of the numbers written on the edges of the path. Let's denote the obtained xor as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then take an arbitrary interesting triple ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":",  "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") and any path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", with the xor of the numbers on it equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Consider the cycle of the following form: let's go through the first path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", and then through the reversed direction of the second path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Thus, we will get a cycle containing the vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", with the xor of the numbers on it equals to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0eb0a68abf1ac29c1ae19f63a53588231bc145c446d2288ed55b65716ed95c3d.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"On the other hand, we can take any cycle which starts and ends in the vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", with the xor of the numbers on it equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", and insert it before the path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Thus, we will get the path "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", with the xor of the numbers on it equals to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/cc2b899ffc826206d73cd5b42b77de682b153f5e77b645cf90cd6fae26b56459.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", i.e. we will get an interesting triple "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/a84eca59fba198b9fc385c83aaa9d54bd4e59ef1efa3282315e1a299d38d9ae5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note that in the same manner we can add cycle, that doesn't pass through the vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". To do this, we can find any path from the vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to some of the vertices of the cycle, then go through the cycle itself, and then add the edges of same path in reversed order. As a result, all the edges of this path will be included in the resulting path twice and will not affect the resulting xor."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"From this we get the following idea: for a fixed pair of vertices, we can find some path between them, and then add different cycles to it to obtain all interesting triples."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Basis of the cycle space of the graph can be obtained as follows: we find some spanning tree, and then for each edge, which is not included in that tree, add the cycle containing this edge and the path in the tree connecting its endpoints to the basis. For each cycle of the basis we will find xor of the numbers written in its edges."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now, let's move from the operation of taking xor of numbers to the operations with vectors. To do this, we can represent all the numbers in the graph in the form of vectors of length 60, where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th element of the vector will be equal to the value of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th bit in this number. Then taking xor of two numbers will be reduced to the addition of two vectors over the modulo 2. As a result, we can move from cycle space of the graph to the space "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0f68288e604344adfca7b5366d236de04471d14b894cb0f225e1034b74eb33df.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A linear combination of cycles of the graph, gives us a linear combination of vectors corresponding to xor of the numbers on the edges of the cycles. The set of linear combinations of the vectors form a subspace of "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0f68288e604344adfca7b5366d236de04471d14b894cb0f225e1034b74eb33df.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". We can find the basis of this subspace using Gaussian elimination."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's denote the dimension of the basis as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then, for every pair of vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", the amount of interest triples for these vertices is equal to 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", and all values of the parameter "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"s"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for these triples can be obtained by adding the vector corresponding to an arbitrary path between these vertices to the described subspace."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's choose a pair of vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and some bit "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and count how many times the value of 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" will appear in the resulting sum. Let's assume that the value of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th bit on the path between the chosen vertices is equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th bit is equal to one for at least one vector of the basis, then this bit will be equal to one in the 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" triples. Otherwise, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th bit in all the 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" triples will be equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's start the depth first search from some vertex fixed vertex "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and find a paths to each vertex of the graph. For each of this paths we calculate the value of xor of the numbers on them. For each bit "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" let's count how many times it was equal to zero and one on these paths (denote this numbers "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"zero"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"one"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then, for a fixed bit "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", for exactly "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"zero"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"one"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" pairs of vertices it will be equal to one on the path connecting this vertices, and for "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/561493a0f93a9060a95453a2231d4b79b68fbb520e30fab75968f576dd200f47.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" pairs it will be zero."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Thus, if the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th bit is equal to one in at least one vector from the basis, we should add "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3a0d388897ec6f1be7e9f4ef025fd650b3bc399edf104a69932c93277c227969.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" to the answer. In the other case, if bits "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is equal to zero for all vectors from the basis, we should add 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"zero"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"one"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * 2"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"r"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" to the answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The resulting complexity of this solution is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"log"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"18"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/47644"}